IELTS.visuals({"t01m.js":[[["A strong, quiet performance that is held back by one habit, not by any weakness.",null]],[["She speaks at length without visible effort and the long turn is clearly shaped — the room, how often, what she does there, then the real reason, then the chair — without the bullets being read out.","chair"],["The chair arrives last and is the best thing in the test, which means she was still building while she spoke.","chair"],["What keeps this at 7 rather than 8 is the Part 3 openings: five of the six answers carry 'I'd say' or 'I think' in the first clause, and by the third the examiner can hear the pattern.","exam"],["A marker used five times is no longer range.",null]],[["This is the strongest of her four.",null],["The vocabulary is not showy — there is barely a long word in the whole transcript — but it is precise, and twice she builds a phrase rather than retrieving one.",null],["'Nobody needs anything from me in that room' is the whole long turn compressed into nine words, and 'stops being a luxury' is an idiom used at exactly the right weight, which is what the band 8 descriptor means by using idiom naturally.",null]],[["A real range — a first conditional, relative clauses, present perfect used correctly and unprompted, and frequent error-free sentences.","gift"],["It sits at 7 rather than 8 because the complexity is nearly all built the same way: a main clause, then 'so' or 'because' or a relative clause, and almost never the other order.",null],["The structures are correct and the RANGE of ways she subordinates is narrower than her vocabulary is.",null]],[["Easy to follow throughout.",null],["The pausing is purposeful — she stops where a listener needs her to, and the long turn divides into units a listener can hold.",null],["Clear L1 traces remain in the vowel of words like 'hospital' and in a slightly rolled r; neither costs anything, because neither makes the listener work.","hospital"],["Accent is not the criterion.",null],["(A transcript cannot carry everything an examiner hears, so this is the one band on this page you should treat as the least certain.)","exam"]],[["One change.",null],["Vary the openings in Part 3.",null],["The content of those six answers is already band 8 — the problem is that five of them arrive through the same door.","door"],["Replace two of the 'I'd say' openings with a concession ('There's a lot in that, although…') and a flat disagreement, and Fluency and Coherence goes to 8, which takes the overall to 8.0.",null],["Nothing else in this test needs touching.","exam"]]],"t01p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Lama Haddad.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Lama is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Lama.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about where you live.",null]],[["Do you live in a house or a flat?","home"]],[["I live in a flat in Qanawat, which is in the old part of Damascus.","map"],["We're on the third floor and there's no lift.","stairs"],["The ceilings are very high, so the rooms look nice but they're really cold in winter.","winter"]],[["What do you like most about living there?",null]],[["Well, I like that I can walk everywhere.",null],["The shops and the souk are about fifteen minutes away, so I don't need a taxi.","souk"],["Honestly, after four years here it's strange to wait for one.",null]],[["Is there anything you would change about it?",null]],[["I'd change the noise, to be honest.",null],["We're above a busy street in Qanawat and it starts at six in the morning.","street"],["But that's the price of living in the centre, so I don't complain much.","money"]],[["And do you think you'll live in the same area in ten years?",null]],[["I hope so, because I really like the area.",null],["It's going to depend on my work, though.",null],["If I get a job in a hospital on the other side of Damascus, I'll probably have to move closer to it.","hospital"]],[["Let's move on to talk about studying.",null]],[["What are you studying at the moment?",null]],[["I'm studying medicine, and I'm in my fourth year at Damascus University, which means three mornings a week I'm at the teaching hospital and the rest of the time I'm in lectures.","university"],["So it's quite a full week.","calendar"]],[["Why did you choose that subject?",null]],[["Well, there wasn't one big reason.",null],["I was good at biology at school, and my aunt is a children's doctor, so I spent a lot of holidays helping in her clinic in Damascus.","hospital"],["After that it just felt normal.",null]],[["Do you prefer studying alone or with other people?",null]],[["I prefer to study alone, actually, because I can't concentrate when someone's talking.",null],["But before an exam at the university I want a friend to ask me questions out loud.","exam"],["I need to hear myself say the answer.",null]],[["What's the hardest part of your studies?",null]],[["Honestly, the speed is the hard part.",null],["You learn something well on Monday, and by Thursday there's three new things.","calendar"],["I'd say you never really feel ready at the hospital, and you have to accept that.","hospital"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about sleep.","night"]],[["How many hours do you usually sleep?","clock"]],[["About six hours, usually.","clock"],["My mother says it isn't enough, and she's probably right.","family"],["When I've got a week at the hospital it's even less, so I'm always tired by Thursday.","calendar"]],[["Do you ever take a nap during the day?","heart_health"]],[["Yes, quite often, actually.",null],["I'll sleep for twenty minutes in the hospital library between a shift and a lecture, because everyone there does it and nobody's going to say anything about it.","hospital"]],[["Has the amount you sleep changed in recent years?","night"]],[["It's less regular now, I'd say.",null],["Going to bed at midnight every night would be fine.","night"],["The problem is that one night I sleep at nine and the next night at two, and that's what makes me tired at the hospital.","night"]],[["And do you think people today sleep less than in the past?","night"]],[["Probably, but it depends who you're comparing them with.",null],["My grandmother got up at five every morning all her life, and nobody here called that a problem.","family"],["Her day just started early and it didn't finish late.","clock"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t01p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a place where you like to spend time.",null]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I'd like to talk about the library on the fourth floor of the teaching hospital.","library"],["It's quite a plain room, but I go there three or four times a week.","calendar"]],[["I usually go between a morning shift and an afternoon lecture, when I've got two hours and nothing to do.","hospital"],["At first I went because I had nowhere else to wait.",null],["Now I go because I want to.",null]],[["I don't do very much there, to be honest.",null],["Sometimes I read, but usually I just sit by the window with a coffee and I don't study at all.","chair"],["In a hospital that's quite hard to find.","hospital"]],[["The reason I like it is that nobody needs anything from me in that room.",null],["Everywhere else in the building someone asks me a question and often I can't answer it.","stairs"]],[["In the library nobody expects anything, so I can stop for a while.","library"]],[["There's also an old chair by the window, and you can see Mount Qasioun from it on a clear day.","chair"]],[["We call it Doctor Haddad's chair, because a professor sat in it every afternoon for about twenty years.","chair"]],[["He left before I started and nobody sits in it now.",null],["We all sit around it instead, which sounds silly, but I'd be upset if someone used it.","chair"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you usually go there alone?",null]],[["Always alone, yes.",null],["Two people from my year go as well, but we agreed not to talk in there.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t01p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a place where you like to spend time, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.",null]],[["Let's consider homes and cities.","map"]],[["Do you think it's better to live in the centre of a city or outside it?","map"]],[["Well, it depends on your day, I think.",null],["If you work in one place, outside the city is better and much quieter.","map"],["For me it isn't possible, because I'm at three different buildings in Damascus every week.","calendar"]],[["How has housing in your country changed over the last generation?","generations"]],[["Families are smaller now, and I'd say the flats changed with them.","family"],["My grandmother's flat in Damascus has got one very big room and small bedrooms, because three generations lived there together.","map"],["New flats are the opposite.",null]],[["What kind of housing do you think people will want in the future?",null]],[["I think people will want smaller places that still work when the services stop.",null],["Here we're already asking about the water tank and the solar panel before we even look at the kitchen, and I'd expect that to spread.","solar"]],[["Now let's talk about public space.",null]],[["Some people say cities don't have enough public space.","map"],["Would you agree?",null]],[["I agree, although we have got parks in Damascus.","map"],["The problem is that there aren't many places where you can sit for two hours without buying something, and I think that's important for people with no money.","chair"]],[["Who benefits most from public spaces?",null]],[["Old men and young children, I'd say, if you look at who's actually there.",null],["In the square near us in Damascus at five in the afternoon it's grandfathers and small children, because everyone else is still at work.","map"]],[["Will public spaces become more or less important in the future?",null]],[["More important, I think.",null],["Flats are getting smaller in Damascus and more people are working at home, so they need somewhere to go.","map"],["I'd say the park stops being a luxury when that happens, and it's starting to matter for everybody.","park"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t02m.js":[[["A candidate whose English is better than his delivery lets the examiner hear.","exam"]],[["He fills the two minutes without strain and the long turn has a real structure: the claim, the person, the failure, the resolution.","clock"],["The repetition of 'about thirty times' is deliberate and rhetorical, not a stall — that distinction matters, and an examiner hears it.","exam"],["He is held at 7 by short, additive runs in Part 1: several answers are three simple sentences in a row with no connective between them.",null],["It reads as a style, and it is, but the descriptor asks for a range of connectives and he mostly uses the full stop.",null]],[["Enough range to say everything he wants, with some real precision in his own field, and two self-built phrases that are better than anything he could have memorised.",null],["What keeps it at 7 is reach: outside work his vocabulary thins noticeably.",null],["In the Part 3 answers about education he falls back on general words — thing, worse, enough — where a more precise item was available.",null],["The resource is deep in one place and shallow a step away from it.",null]],[["Frequent error-free sentences and a genuine range when he is engaged: the conditional above is embedded mid-sentence with an aside inside it, which is a confident piece of construction.",null],["Two slips in the whole test, neither of which obscures meaning.","exam"],["He does not reach 8 because a majority of his sentences are simple by choice; the complex ones are correct but are a minority.",null]],[["Comfortable to listen to, with stress used purposefully in reported speech and the pace varied for emphasis.",null],["Individual sounds carry clear L1 features — the vowel in 'floor', a devoiced final consonant on several words — and none of it interferes.",null],["He stays at 7 rather than 8 because intonation flattens across his longer additive sentences: three statements at the same contour in a row, which makes a listener work slightly to find where the emphasis was meant to be.",null]],[["One change, and it is the same change in all four criteria: join his sentences.",null],["Hadi's content is consistently band 7 or above and his delivery is chopped into three-sentence blocks with nothing between them.",null],["Taking the Part 1 answers and linking roughly one pair in each with a which-clause, a so-that clause or an even-though clause would raise Fluency AND Grammar together, because the subordination and the connective range are the same fix.",null],["That is 7.5 overall without him learning a single new word.",null]]],"t02p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Hadi Mansour.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Hadi is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Hadi.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about work.",null]],[["Do you work or are you a student?",null]],[["I work.",null],["I'm a site engineer, and I'm on a residential job in Homs at the moment, six floors.","map"],["It's probably eighteen months from finished if nothing goes wrong, which it will.","calendar"]],[["What do you do on a typical day at work?",null]],[["Look, most of it is checking.",null],["I walk the floors in Homs in the morning and I check what was poured yesterday actually matches the drawing, and then I'm on the phone all afternoon to people who are late.","map"],["Basically I'm paid to notice things early.","money"]],[["Do you enjoy your job?","office"]],[["Most days, yes.",null],["The part I like is that at the end of it there's a building.","stairs"],["I mean, you can walk past it in Homs in ten years and it's still standing, and not many jobs give you that.","map"]],[["Is there anything you'd change about it?",null]],[["The waiting.",null],["You can lose three weeks in Homs because one delivery didn't arrive, and there's nothing you can do with that time.","calendar"],["To be honest that's harder than the actual work.",null]],[["Let's move on to talk about daily routine.",null]],[["What time do you usually get up?",null]],[["Half five, quarter to six.",null],["Site starts at seven and the drive into Homs is about forty minutes if the road's clear.","car"],["Honestly, I've never once needed an alarm on a working day, but I do oversleep at the weekend.","calendar"]],[["Do you prefer mornings or evenings?",null]],[["Mornings, easily.",null],["Put it this way: by six in the evening I'm no use to anyone.","night"],["Everything difficult I do before eleven.",null]],[["Has your routine changed in the last few years?",null]],[["It's got more regular, which I didn't expect.",null],["There was a long stretch in Homs when nothing was predictable, not even the power, and now the fact that I know what Tuesday looks like is worth something.","map"],["I suppose I wouldn't have said that five years ago.",null]],[["Do you ever change your routine at the weekend?","calendar"]],[["Completely.",null],["I don't set anything, I don't go near the site, and there's football on Friday afternoon with the same eight men I've played with since school.","calendar"],["That's basically the whole plan.",null]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about free time.",null]],[["What do you do in your free time?",null]],[["Football on Friday, and not much else that's organised.","calendar"],["Look, by the time I get home it's seven and I've been on my feet since half six in the morning.","home"],["Free time is mostly sitting down.","chair"]],[["Do you prefer to spend free time alone or with other people?",null]],[["With people, but quietly.",null],["I don't want to go anywhere.",null],["Four of us sitting in somebody's flat in Homs with the television on and nobody really watching it, that's basically my ideal evening.","chair"]],[["Have your hobbies changed since you were younger?",null]],[["I used to fix things that weren't broken.",null],["Motorbikes, mostly.",null],["I mean, I still would, but the flat in Homs is small and my wife's got views about engine parts in the kitchen, which to be fair are reasonable views.","map"]],[["Do you think people have enough free time?",null]],[["Depends on the job, mostly, I'd say.","office"],["On site you get what the contract says and that's it.",null],["The men I work with in Homs don't talk about free time as a thing you have.","map"],["They talk about Friday.","calendar"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t02p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first.",null]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I'll talk about reading construction drawings properly, which sounds like nothing and took me about a year.",null]],[["This was my first year on site, in Homs, straight out of university.","map"],["And the thing is, I could read a drawing.",null]],[["I'd done four years of it and I could tell you what every line meant.",null],["What I was not able to do was look at a set of drawings and see the building.","stairs"]],[["How I learned it was a foreman called Abu Rami, who had no degree at all and could do it instantly.","university"]],[["He'd look at a sheet for about ten seconds and say, that won't work, the pipe has to come through there.",null],["And he was always right.",null]],[["I followed him around for two weeks asking him how he knew, and he could not explain it, which was no help to me at all.","calendar"]],[["Why it was hard, I think, is because nobody teaches it.",null],["They teach you the symbols.","school"]],[["Nobody teaches you to hold four sheets in your head at once and notice that the electrical drawing and the structural drawing disagree.",null],["That's not a skill you can be told.",null]],[["You have to get it wrong about thirty times, and I got it wrong about thirty times.",null]],[["Now I do what Abu Rami did, more or less.",null],["And I still can't explain it to the new ones either, which I suppose is fair.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you teach that skill to younger engineers now?","school"]],[["Honestly, I try.",null],["Mostly I'll just put them in front of a problem on the Homs site and let them find it, because that's the only way it went in for me.","map"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t02p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a skill you learned that was difficult at first, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.",null]],[["Let's consider learning at work.",null]],[["Do people learn more from training courses or from experience at work?",null]],[["Experience, I'd say, for the kind of work I do.",null],["A course gives you the rules, which you need, and I'm not against them.",null],["But a course can't give you the feeling that something's wrong before you can say why, and on a site in Homs that feeling is most of the job.","map"]],[["Should companies be responsible for training their workers?",null]],[["Up to a point, yes.",null],["The argument against is obvious, of course: you train somebody and they leave, and I've seen it happen in Homs.","station"],["But the alternative is a company full of people who only know what they walked in with, and that's worse for everyone including the ones who stay.",null]],[["Are some skills impossible to teach?","school"]],[["I'd say the ones that are really judgement, yes.",null],["Take what I was describing on the Homs job.","map"],["Abu Rami genuinely could not explain how he did it, and he wasn't hiding anything.",null],["You can put someone next to it for long enough that they catch it, and that's not the same as teaching it.","school"]],[["Now let's talk about education and age.",null]],[["Is it harder to learn new things as you get older?",null]],[["Harder to start, I'd say, not harder to do.",null],["At thirty-one I'm better at learning something than I was at twenty at university, because I know what I don't know.","university"],["What I've lost is being ready to look stupid in front of people, and honestly that's what stops me.",null]],[["Do you think people will need to retrain more often in the future?",null]],[["Probably, and I'd guess in my trade it will come through the materials.",null],["Everything I learned about concrete in Homs still applies.","map"],["If that changes, and it might, because there's a lot of pressure on it, then half of what I know goes.",null]],[["How is education in your country different from your parents' time?","family"]],[["More people get through it, I think, and it's worth less on its own.",null],["My father had a secondary certificate and that was enough to get a government job.","family"],["Now everyone my age has a degree, and the degree isn't the thing that gets you hired.","university"],["Who you worked for is.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t03m.js":[[["The same candidate as Test 1, six weeks later, half a band higher — and the whole difference is visible in the first three words of each Part 3 answer.","exam"]],[["The long turn develops without a single prompt and lands on a point it had been building towards from the first sentence — the handing over — which the candidate never announces.","exam"],["The short-short-long rhythm in the fourth paragraph is control, not accident.",null],["In Part 3 she opens six answers six different ways, which is precisely the habit that held her at 7 in Test 1 and is now fixed.","exam"],["Hesitation appears only when she is choosing a thought, never a word.",null]],[["Wide and used with real precision, and the mark of band 8 here is not rare vocabulary but construction: three times she builds a phrase to fit a thought that had no ready phrase.",null],["'Nobody announces it, they just let you' is the whole long turn compressed into eight words.",null],["Idiom is natural and correctly weighted throughout, including the risk of 'almost something you have to admit to' about a vegetable, which could have misfired and does not.",null]],[["A wide range used flexibly, with the great majority of sentences error-free across a long transcript.",null],["The second example carries a modal, a perfect passive and a coordinated contrast inside one sentence, and it is produced at speed.",null],["The first builds a comparative out of a counterfactual while she is still speaking, which is not a structure anybody memorises.",null],["She is not at 9 because the range, though wide, is deployed on a narrow set of hinges: and, because, which.",null]],[["Sustained and effortless to follow.",null],["The pause before the key clause is the clearest evidence here: it is rhetorical, placed to give a listener time, and it is the thing that most distinguishes a band 8 speaker from a band 7 one who simply does not stop.",null],["L1 features persist and are irrelevant at this level — she is never unclear, and clarity is the entire criterion.",null],["(A transcript cannot carry everything an examiner hears, so treat this band as the least certain on the page.)","exam"]],[["There is no single change that makes this a 9, and candidates should not chase one.",null],["The honest note is that the gap between this test and Test 1, taken six weeks earlier by the same candidate on the same preparation, is ONE habit: she stopped opening every Part 3 answer the same way.","exam"],["That is what half a band looks like.",null],["It is not more vocabulary and it is not harder grammar.",null]]],"t03p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Lama Haddad.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Lama is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Lama.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about food and cooking.","meal"]],[["Do you enjoy cooking?",null]],[["I do, though I think I'm better at feeding people than at cooking for them.",null],["I can make about six things properly, and I make all six far too often, so my mother knows exactly what's coming.","family"]],[["Who usually cooks in your home?","home"]],[["My mother, and she'd say that's because nobody else can, which isn't quite fair but isn't entirely unfair either.","family"],["Mind you, she's never actually let anyone try for long enough to find out.",null]],[["Has the food you eat changed since you were a child?","meal"]],[["Less than it should have, I think.",null],["The one real change is that we eat later.","meal"],["In Damascus everything moved when the evenings became the only reliable part of the day, and a meal at ten at night isn't the same meal even when it's the same food.","meal"]],[["Is there any food you really dislike?","meal"]],[["Aubergine, which in Syria is almost something you have to admit to.","bag_of_shopping"],["It's in everything, actually.",null],["I've spent my whole life quietly moving it to the side of the plate, and I don't think anyone has ever noticed.",null]],[["Let's move on to talk about weekends.",null]],[["What do you usually do at the weekend?","calendar"]],[["Friday is my grandmother's, and of course that isn't negotiable.","calendar"],["Everyone goes, lunch is at two and nobody leaves before five, and I've stopped pretending I have a say in it.","meal"],["Saturday I catch up on everything Friday cost me.","calendar"]],[["Do you prefer to spend the weekend at home or going out?","calendar"]],[["At home, at the moment.","home"],["I keep checking whether that's what I actually want or whether the hospital has just worn me out, and I think it's the first one.","hospital"],["Ask me again after exams and you might get a different answer.","exam"]],[["Are weekends different now from when you were a child?",null]],[["Much quieter, mainly because the family's smaller than it was.",null],["The same Friday lunch happens in the same room with about half the people, and nobody ever mentions it.","calendar"],["I think that's harder than if they did.",null]],[["Do you think people should work at the weekend?","calendar"]],[["If the work genuinely needs it, then of course yes.",null],["What I'd argue with is the assumption that it does.",null],["Half the weekend work I see at the hospital exists because somebody didn't plan Wednesday properly.","calendar"],["That isn't an emergency.",null],["It's a habit.",null]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about photographs.","photograph"]],[["Do you take many photographs?","photograph"]],[["Far too many, and I don't look at any of them, which I think is normal now.",null],["There are about four thousand of them on my phone.","phone"],["There's maybe nine I could describe, and most of those are from one Friday lunch.","calendar"]],[["Do you prefer taking photos of people or places?","photograph"]],[["People, although I'm honestly bad at it, because I always wait until everyone is ready and the ready ones are never the good ones.",null],["The photographs I keep are all of people at Friday lunch who hadn't noticed me.","photograph"]],[["Do you ever print your photographs?","photograph"]],[["Almost never, and I think my grandmother finds that genuinely upsetting.","family"],["She's got an album with the dates written underneath in pencil, and mine only exist on a phone, which she doesn't really count as existing.","notebook"]],[["Why do you think people like looking at old photographs?","photograph"]],[["I think it's about proof more than memory.",null],["A photograph shows that the version of you who existed then really did exist, and nobody needs that until they're old enough to start doubting it.","photograph"],["My grandmother's album is mostly that.","photograph"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t03p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a meal you still remember.","meal"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["It was at my grandmother's, about two years ago, on the Friday after I finished my second year exams.","calendar"],["Yabrak, so vine leaves with rice and meat in them.","meal"]],[["That matters, because it isn't a dish anyone makes in a hurry.","meal"]],[["It takes three people and most of a morning, and that's really the point of it.",null]],[["There were three of us rolling: my grandmother, my aunt and me.","family"]],[["What made it unusual is that it was the first time I'd been allowed to do it properly, in the row that gets served.",null]],[["I was slow and about a third of mine came out the wrong shape, and my grandmother watched me do every one of them and said nothing.","family"]],[["That's why I remember it.",null],["She isn't a gentle woman about food.","meal"],["She has corrected my aunt, who is fifty, in front of guests.","family"]],[["And she sat there and let me put badly rolled vine leaves into the pot that everyone was going to eat from, which in her terms is an enormous thing to do.","chair"]],[["Nobody said anything about it at lunch.","meal"],["Somebody probably got one of the bad ones.",null]],[["I've thought since that this is how most things get handed over in a family.","family"],["Nobody announces it, they just let you.",null],["I'd have been embarrassed if she'd made a speech about it.",null]],[["So she made room instead, and then we ate.","meal"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you make that dish yourself now?","meal"]],[["Twice.",null],["Both times badly, and both times with my grandmother on the phone from Damascus telling me what I'd done wrong, which I think she enjoys more than she'd enjoy the cooking.","family"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t03p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a meal you still remember, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","meal"]],[["Let's consider food and family.","family"]],[["Do families eat together as often as they used to?","family"]],[["Less often, although I'm not sure that means what people think it means.",null],["The daily meal has mostly gone, yes.","meal"],["The Friday one matters enormously more now, because it's the only one left, so I don't think the total amount of family has actually fallen.","calendar"],["It's just all in one place.",null]],[["Why is food so important in family traditions?","meal"]],[["Because eating is the one thing that has to happen anyway.","meal"],["You can skip a holiday, but you can't skip dinner.","meal"],["So anything a family wants to pass on gets attached to the meal, because the meal is guaranteed to come round again.","meal"],["That's why the recipe is never really about the recipe.",null],["Nobody needs three people to roll vine leaves.",null],["It's a meeting with food in it.","meal"]],[["Some people say cooking traditional food takes too much time.","meal"],["Would you agree?",null]],[["It does take too long, and I'd say that's the function and not a fault.",null],["Having said that, I can't pretend the women in my grandmother's generation all chose to.","generations"],["Something can be worth keeping and still have been unfair while it was happening, and I think my generation is allowed to keep the first part of that.","generations"]],[["Now let's talk about how eating is changing.","meal"]],[["How has the way people eat changed in the last twenty years?","meal"]],[["I'd say the timing has changed more than the food has, where I live.","meal"],["People eat later, and in shifts, because the day doesn't have the shape it used to have.","meal"],["My grandmother's generation ate at two because two was when everyone was free.","generations"],["Nobody is free at the same time now.",null]],[["Will traditional dishes still be made in fifty years?",null]],[["Made, yes.",null],["Made at home, I'm less sure.","home"],["I think they'll go the way bread went.","meal"],["Everybody's grandmother baked, nobody's mother does, and nobody thinks bread has disappeared.","family"],["It's just made by somebody else now, and something did go when that happened, even if it's hard to say what.",null]],[["Is it a problem that people eat more processed food?","meal"]],[["It depends whether you mean health or culture, and I think those two arguments get mixed up all the time.",null],["The health case is simple enough.",null],["The other one is weaker than people assume, because a tin isn't the end of anything, and plenty of what we call traditional was itself a shortcut that somebody's great-grandmother would have disapproved of.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t04m.js":[[["The best structuring in the book, undone twice by the same habit in the last four words of a sentence.",null]],[["The long turn is the strongest piece of structuring in the first four tests.","exam"],["She announces a deliberately unpromising choice, defers the reason, and lands it — the return to 'an exchange rate app' at the end closes a loop she opened two minutes earlier.","clock"],["That is topic development without any prompting at all.",null],["Hesitation is entirely for content.",null],["Her 'right?' tag appears three times and is conversational rather than a stall, so it costs nothing.","money"]],[["Ready and precise, with more risk-taking than anyone else in the book and a high success rate on it.",null],["The second example is the clearest band 8 evidence in this test: she rejects the obvious judgement and constructs a more exact one in the next breath.","exam"],["Idiomatic language is natural throughout and her paraphrase under pressure is quick — when 'exchange rate' nearly escapes her in Part 1 she routes round it in half a second without the listener noticing a gap.",null]],[["This is the criterion that holds her at 7.5 overall, and it is purely a speed problem.",null],["The range is there — second conditionals, a subjunctive 'were', embedded clauses, a correctly handled 'it'll separate into two different things that we're currently calling the same thing'.",null],["But she speaks faster than she constructs, and across the test there are five concord or article slips of exactly the same kind, all in long sentences, none in short ones.","exam"],["Frequent error-free sentences, but not a majority.",null]],[["Clear and engaging, with good contrastive stress on the pairs she builds.",null],["What keeps her at 7 is pace: when she is enthusiastic — which is most of the time — the ends of her longer sentences speed up and quieten, and twice in this test the final three or four words are genuinely hard to catch.","exam"],["The sounds are not the issue.",null],["The management of the breath across a long sentence is.",null]],[["Slow the last five words of every long sentence.",null],["It sounds trivial and it is worth a full band here, because the same habit is costing her twice: the grammar slips are all in the accelerated part of long sentences, and the pronunciation lapses are in exactly the same place.",null],["Rawan does not need more language.",null],["She needs to finish her sentences at the speed she started them, and that single change takes both Grammar and Pronunciation to 8 — and the overall to 8.0.",null]]],"t04p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Rawan Saleh.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Rawan is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Rawan.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about technology.",null]],[["Do you use a computer every day?","laptop"]],[["Every day, all day.",null],["It's my actual job, I'm a designer.","office"],["So if the laptop dies I don't have a profession, and with the power in Damascus that's a slightly alarming thing to notice out loud.","laptop"]],[["What's the most useful piece of technology you own?",null]],[["The laptop, obviously, but actually I'd say the power bank.","laptop"],["People think that's a joke and it isn't.",null],["When the electricity goes in the middle of a call with a client in Germany, the thing that saves the job isn't the computer.","solar"]],[["Has technology changed the way you work?",null]],[["It is the way I work.",null],["The thing is, I've never had a job that wasn't like this.","office"],["I've never been in an office and I've never met most of the people who pay me, so I can't really compare.","office"],["I've worked this way since university.","university"],["This is just what work is.",null]],[["Are there any disadvantages to it?",null]],[["Oh, loads.",null],["The obvious one is that you never stop, because there's no building you leave.","stairs"],["But the one nobody talks about, I think, is that you're completely alone with your own judgement.",null],["Nobody in my building is going to walk past your screen and say that's not working.","stairs"]],[["Let's move on to talk about mobile phones.","phone"]],[["How often do you use your phone?","phone"]],[["Constantly, and I've stopped pretending otherwise.",null],["I did one of those weeks where you check the screen time and then feel terrible about it, and honestly nothing changed except that now I know the number.","calendar"],["It's worse on the days the power's out, which is most of them.",null]],[["What do you use it for most?",null]],[["Work, annoyingly.",null],["If it were all friends and photos I'd feel better about it.","friends"],["It's clients, invoices and chasing payment, so the office comes with me everywhere, including Friday lunch at my mother's.","office"]],[["Could you manage without a phone for a week?","calendar"]],[["Manage, yes.",null],["Afford to, no.",null],["A week with no phone means a week of not answering clients, and the thing about freelancing in Damascus is that silence costs you the next job, not just this one.","calendar"],["So I've never tried it and I don't plan to.",null]],[["Do you think children should have phones?",null]],[["I don't know, honestly, and I don't trust anybody who's certain about it.",null],["My sister's twelve and has one, and the arguments against are all completely true, and so is the fact that my mother can find her.","family"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about neighbours.","stairs"]],[["Do you know your neighbours well?","stairs"]],[["Honestly, better than I'd choose to.",null],["It's one stairwell and five flats in our building, so everyone knows when anyone's internet is down, because we all complain at the same time on the stairs.","stairs"]],[["Do you think it's important to have good neighbours?","stairs"]],[["More important here than people from somewhere else would assume, I think.",null],["The thing is, a lot of what makes a building work isn't official.","stairs"],["Who pays the generator man, who has the key to the roof.","solar"],["That only works if people are speaking, and here they're speaking all the time.",null]],[["How have neighbourhoods changed since you were a child?",null]],[["People move more, so you don't get the twenty-year neighbour any more.","stairs"],["Actually that cuts both ways.",null],["My mother could tell you the history of every family on her street, and some of those families would rather she couldn't.","family"]],[["Would you prefer to live somewhere quieter?",null]],[["I say yes about once a week and honestly I'd last a fortnight.","calendar"],["I've tried working somewhere silent, at my aunt's place outside the city, and I got nothing done, which was humiliating and quite useful to find out.","map"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t04p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe an app or a website you use a lot.","phone"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I'm going to pick a boring one, because the honest answer isn't the design software.","laptop"],["It's a currency app.","money"],["Just an exchange rate app, nothing clever.","phone"]],[["I started using it about two years ago, after a client abroad paid me in dollars and the money reached Damascus in lira, and I couldn't work out whether the payment was short or whether the rate had moved.","money"]],[["And that's a bad position to be in, right?",null],["Because you either say nothing and feel cheated, or you accuse somebody of something and you're wrong.",null],["I just didn't have the information.",null]],[["Now I check it before I quote, when I invoice, and when the money lands.","money"],["Three times per job, every job.","office"]],[["And I also check it at eleven at night for no reason at all, which isn't healthy.","night"]],[["The reason it's actually useful, though, isn't the money.","money"],["It's that it lets me have the conversation.",null]],[["If a payment comes in short I can say, on this date the rate was this, so I think something's gone wrong.",null]],[["That's a completely different conversation from saying I think you've paid me less.","money"]],[["One of them is a fact and one of them is an accusation, and when the client is four thousand kilometres away and you've never met them, the difference between those two is your whole career.",null]],[["So, an exchange rate app.","phone"],["It's the least interesting thing on my phone and it's the one I'd replace first.","phone"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you recommend it to other people?",null]],[["To other freelancers, constantly.",null],["Everybody I know who works like this has had the same bad week, they just had it on their own.","calendar"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t04p3.js":[[["We've been talking about an app or a website you use a lot, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","laptop"]],[["Let's consider technology and work.",null]],[["How has technology changed the kind of work people do?",null]],[["It's changed who you're competing with more than what you do, I'd say.",null],["The design I do isn't different from design twenty years ago.","laptop"],["But twenty years ago I'd have been competing with people in Damascus, and now it's everybody with a laptop.","laptop"],["That cuts both ways, obviously.",null],["It's also why I have clients at all.",null]],[["Some people say technology has made work more stressful.",null],["Would you agree?",null]],[["Yes, though usually for the wrong reason.",null],["It isn't the volume.",null],["It's that the boundary went, so there's no moment where work is over and it's never over.",null],["Having said that, I chose this, and most people I know in Damascus didn't get to choose anything about their work at all.","map"]],[["Will more people work from home in the future?","home"]],[["More, yes, but I think it'll separate into two different things that we're currently calling the same thing.",null],["There's working from home because your company allows it, and there's working from home because you have no company.","home"],["Almost nobody I know in Damascus is in the first group, and my guess is we end up with different words for them.","map"]],[["Now let's talk about information and trust.",null]],[["Is it harder to know what information to trust now?",null]],[["Harder, or just more obvious that it always was?",null],["I'd say the second, mostly.",null],["My father trusted one newspaper and one television channel completely, and that wasn't better information.","family"],["There was simply less visible disagreement.",null],["What's genuinely new is how much of it there is.",null]],[["How do people decide what to believe online?","laptop"]],[["Whoever says it first and confidently, which is a terrible system.",null],["Take anything about the electricity.","solar"],["There'll be an official number, and there'll be what everyone in the building actually experiences, and people believe the building.","stairs"],["Which is often right, and still isn't reasoning.",null]],[["Should companies be responsible for the information on their platforms?",null]],[["Partly, and I say that reluctantly, because I don't love the idea of a company deciding what's true.",null],["But the alternative is pretending they're only a pipe, and they aren't.",null],["They choose what gets seen, and so does everybody forwarding it on the street.","street"],["If you choose, you're responsible.",null],["You can't have the first without the second.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t05m.js":[[["The same candidate as Test 2, six weeks later, half a band lower — and his vocabulary and pronunciation did not change at all.","exam"]],[["This is where the half band went, and it is almost all of it.",null],["He can still speak at length — the long turn is a full two minutes and never stalls.","clock"],["What fails is coherence under abstraction.",null],["Six of his answers END where they BEGAN: the reason restates the question and the last sentence repeats the first.",null],["'Important' three times in four sentences; 'better' four times in one answer; 'it comes back to the same thing' offered as a conclusion when nothing has been argued.",null],["His connectives collapse to 'so' and 'because' once the topic leaves the ground he knows.",null],["And the final Part 3 answer is abandoned outright — which, on its own, a band 7 candidate does not do.","exam"]],[["Unchanged from Test 2, and the clearest evidence that his ENGLISH did not get worse.","exam"],["On his own ground the resource is precise and occasionally very good — 'I've seen more of it than I'd have chosen to' is a band 8 sentence.",null],["Away from it he reaches and misses: 'and this' is a list he could not complete, and 'the situation' is a word standing in for several he did not retrieve.",null],["That is exactly what band 7 lexical resource looks like — flexible, with attempts at less common items that sometimes do not land.",null],["It did not drop.",null],["The other criteria did.",null]],[["Two different things are happening.",null],["The simple sentences remain accurate: when Hadi says something short, it is right.",null],["But under pressure he starts complex sentences and does not finish them — there are four abandoned subordinate clauses in Part 3 alone, each replaced mid-stream by a simpler restart.",null],["There is also a concord error ('there's too many cars') that he does not catch, where in Test 2 he self-corrected.","exam"],["Attempted complexity with errors that make the listener re-run the sentence is a band 6 description, and this is it.",null]],[["Also unchanged, which again tells you where the problem is not.",null],["Stress is purposeful when he has something to say, pacing is comfortable, and he is never hard to follow.",null],["The filled pauses — 'erm', 'I don't know' — are a fluency matter and are marked there, not here.",null],["Nothing about his pronunciation moved between Test 2 and Test 5.","exam"]],[["Hadi does not need more English.",null],["Two of his four criteria did not move at all between a 7.0 and a 6.5, which should tell every reader of this book something important: HALF A BAND CAN BE ENTIRELY ABOUT WHAT YOU DO WHEN A QUESTION IS HARDER THAN YOU EXPECTED.",null],["The single change is this: when he cannot answer a Part 3 question, he must stop trying to answer it in general and give a specific instead.",null],["He proved he can — the one Part 3 answer that works ('on my site about half the men are from villages an hour or two out') is the one where he reaches for his own site.","clock"],["Four more of those, and Coherence and Grammar both go to 7 — because the specific gives him a sentence he can finish.",null]]],"t05p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Hadi Mansour.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Hadi is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Hadi.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about transport.","bus"]],[["How do you usually travel to work?","station"]],[["I drive.","car"],["It's about forty minutes, maybe fifty if the Hama road is bad.","clock"],["I've got an old car.","car"],["It's fine, it does the job.","office"]],[["Do you enjoy the journey?","station"]],[["It's all right.",null],["I listen to the radio.","music"],["To be honest the first twenty minutes on the Hama road are the best part of my day, because nobody can phone me and I don't have to answer anyone.","clock"]],[["Has transport in your city improved?","bus"]],[["Yeah, it's better.",null],["The roads around Hama are better than they were, basically.","map"],["There's more cars now so sometimes it's worse, but generally it's better.",null],["It's improved.",null]],[["Would you prefer to use public transport?","bus"]],[["No.",null],["I mean, if it was good, maybe.",null],["But it isn't, so no.",null]],[["Let's move on to talk about travelling in your country.","map"]],[["Have you travelled much inside Syria?","map"]],[["A fair bit, yeah.",null],["Work takes me around, basically.",null],["Hama, Tartous once for three months, Damascus when I have to.","map"],["Put it this way, I've seen more of it than I'd have chosen to.",null]],[["Which part of the country do you like most?","map"]],[["The coast.","coast"],["It's green, it's completely different, you come over the mountains and the air changes.","park"],["My wife's family is from near Jableh so we go when we can.","family"]],[["Do you think tourism will grow in your country?","map"]],[["I think, yes, it'll grow.",null],["There's a lot of, of history here, a lot of places, the castles and the Old City in Damascus and this.","map"],["So it could happen, I think it will grow, but it needs the, the situation to be stable, you know, for a long time.",null],["So it depends on that really.",null]],[["Is it important for people to travel inside their own country?","map"]],[["Yes, it's important.",null],["Because you understand the country better, I think.","map"],["If you only know Hama, you don't know Syria.","map"],["So it's important, yes.",null]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about keeping fit.",null]],[["Do you do any sport or exercise?","gym"]],[["Football, Friday.","calendar"],["That's it, really.",null],["My job is physical anyway.","office"],["I'm walking six floors maybe fifteen times a day, so I don't feel guilty about not going to a gym.","gym"]],[["Is it easy to stay fit where you live?",null]],[["Easy enough if you're not fussy.",null],["There's no proper gym near me in Hama, but there's a pitch and there's the hill behind the house.","gym"],["To be honest the facilities aren't the problem.",null],["Everyone's just tired.","heart_health"]],[["Did you play sport when you were at school?","gym"]],[["Same thing, basically.",null],["Football, badly.",null],["At school in Hama I was the one they put at the back because I wouldn't stop running.","map"],["That's not a skill, that's just not being clever enough to stand still.",null]],[["Do you think young people are less active than they used to be?",null]],[["Probably, but I mean, where would they go?",null],["When I was a kid the street was the pitch.","gym"],["Now there are cars parked on it.",null],["It's easy to blame the children for something that's actually about parking.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t05p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a journey that did not go as planned.","station"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["Right, this was going to Tartous, in August, maybe five years ago.","coast"],["Four of us, in my friend Samer's car, which was already a bad decision before we started.","car"]],[["What went wrong was the radiator.",null],["We got maybe two hours out and the temperature gauge went up, and Samer said it's fine, it always does that, which is what he says about everything.","clock"]],[["Twenty minutes later there's steam coming out and we're on the side of the road in, I don't know what the temperature was, forty maybe.","clock"],["No shade.",null],["Nothing.",null]],[["So we waited about three hours.","clock"],["We had, erm, we had one bottle of water between four and we drank it in the first twenty minutes, which was stupid.","water"]],[["A lorry stopped eventually and the driver had water, and he towed us to a place about ten kilometres on where there was a mechanic.","car"]],[["How I felt.",null],["At the time, obviously, angry, because the whole thing was avoidable.",null],["Samer knew about the radiator.",null],["I was angry about that.",null]],[["But also, I don't know, it's the thing everyone talks about now.",null],["Every time the four of us are together somebody mentions it.",null]],[["So in a way it was good in the end, even though at the time it was not good at all.",null]],[["So that's the journey that didn't go to plan.","station"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you still travel with the same friends?","station"]],[["Yeah, all the time.",null],["Not in Samer's car though.","car"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t05p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a journey that did not go as planned, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","station"]],[["Let's consider getting around a city.","map"]],[["What are the main transport problems in big cities?","bus"]],[["Traffic, mainly.","street"],["There's too many cars for the roads in Hama, and the roads weren't built for this many cars, so you get traffic.","map"],["And parking.",null],["Parking is a big problem also, because there's nowhere to put them.",null]],[["How could cities encourage people to use public transport?","map"]],[["Make it better, I suppose.",null],["If the buses are good and they come on time, people will use them.",null],["But if they're not good then people prefer their car, obviously, because it's more comfortable.","car"],["The service taxis work because they come when you want them.","bus"]],[["Should cars be banned from city centres?","map"]],[["That's difficult.",null],["In some cities maybe.","map"],["In Hama the problem is that people need to…","map"],["if you ban the cars, how do people get there?",null],["Unless the public transport is very good.","bus"],["So it comes back to the same thing really, it's the public transport.","bus"]],[["Now let's talk about why people move.",null]],[["Why do people move from the countryside to cities?","map"]],[["Work.",null],["That's most of it.",null],["Look, on my site about half the men are from villages an hour or two out, and they're not in Homs because they like Homs.","map"],["They're there because the job is there.","office"],["Some of them go back every weekend, four hours of travelling, every week.","calendar"]],[["What problems does this movement cause?",null]],[["For the cities, it's pressure.","map"],["More people in the same houses, so the prices go up because nobody is building.","stairs"],["And for the villages around Hama it's the opposite, the young people leave and the village becomes old people only.","mountains"],["Both are a problem but in a different way.",null]],[["Do you think governments should try to stop this movement?",null]],[["Hmm.",null],["I don't think you can stop it, really.",null],["You could maybe, if there was work in the villages near Hama then people would stay, so maybe that.","map"],["But I don't know how you do that.",null],["Yeah, I don't really know.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t06m.js":[[["The widest vocabulary in the book, held to 8.0 by the stress pattern of about six words.",null]],[["Effortless, and developed without a single prompt.",null],["The long turn withholds the point for ninety seconds and then delivers it in one sentence — 'rescuing them is for you and not for them' — which is structural control of a high order.",null],["The mid-sentence break before 'not say anything' is a rhetorical pause that enacts the thing he is describing.",null],["He is not at 9 because in two Part 3 answers he announces the shape before building it ('and then I'd want to argue about what that means'), which is a small amount of visible scaffolding.","stairs"]],[["The strongest single criterion anywhere in this book.",null],["Full flexibility and precision, sustained across eleven minutes, with natural and sophisticated control of idiom.","clock"],["The evidence for 9 rather than 8 is the second quotation: offered the word 'important' by the examiner's own question, he rejects it, supplies a more exact one, and STRESSES it so the listener registers the substitution — then builds the rest of the answer on the distinction.",null],["That is not vocabulary size.",null],["That is using the resource to think with.",null]],[["A wide range, used with full flexibility, and the overwhelming majority of sentences error-free across the longest transcript here.",null],["He uses structures the others do not attempt at all — fronted concessives, an inverted conditional, reported speech nested two deep.",null],["He stays at 8 rather than 9 on a single recurring slip: three times he uses a plural verb after a collective noun in a way that is defensible in British usage but inconsistent within his own speech, twice corrected and once not.",null]],[["This is the criterion holding an otherwise exceptional candidate to 8.0 overall, and it is worth being precise about.","exam"],["He is never unintelligible and his phrasing is masterly — the pause before delivering a point is the best in the book.",null],["But word stress on longer Latinate nouns is unreliable: in eight separate instances the stress lands one syllable too far right, and twice a listener has to back-track to identify the word.",null],["Features of connected speech are otherwise strong.",null],["This is a narrow, fixable, specific fault, and it is costing him a band overall.",null]],[["Six words: arithmetic, comparison, conversation, obligatory, encouragement, information.",null],["Samir's entire gap between 8.0 and 8.5 is the stress pattern of a few dozen long nouns, and those six are the ones he actually reaches for.",null],["Everything else in this test is at or above 8.","exam"],["It is worth saying plainly to every reader: the highest-scoring candidate in this book is held back by PRONUNCIATION, the criterion most candidates assume they can do nothing about and most preparation ignores.","exam"]]],"t06p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Samir Othman.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Samir is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Samir.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about family.","family"]],[["Do you have a large family?","family"]],[["By the standards of my generation, no.","generations"],["By my father's, almost comically small.",null],["He was one of seven and I've got two children, which is most of the story.",null],["You see, the whole arithmetic of a family changed in about forty years and nobody announced it.","family"]],[["Do you spend much time with your family?","family"]],[["More than I did, and that's deliberate.",null],["I spent about fifteen years being the teacher who was always at the school in Damascus, marking, extra lessons, the lot, and I've slowly come round to the view that, honestly, nobody has ever thanked a man for the hours he wasn't at home.","school"]],[["Who are you closest to in your family?","family"]],[["My younger daughter, though I'd never say that in the house.","family"],["She argues with me, which the other one doesn't, and, well, let me put it this way.",null],["I've spent thirty years teaching teenagers in Damascus, and the ones who argued were always the ones I remembered.","map"]],[["Are family relationships changing in your country?","family"]],[["Loosening, I'd say.",null],["I don't think that's the same as weakening.",null],["People are further apart and in touch far more often.",null],["Whether a daily voice message is worth less than a cousin two streets away in Damascus, I genuinely don't know, and nor does anybody who says they do.","family"]],[["Let's move on to talk about friends.","friends"]],[["How often do you see your friends?","friends"]],[["Thursday evenings, almost without fail, which has survived about twenty-five years and, I think, several things that should have ended it.","calendar"],["Four of us, in the same place off the souk.","souk"],["We've run out of new conversation entirely and it doesn't seem to matter at all.",null]],[["Do you prefer a few close friends or a large group?","friends"]],[["A few, and I suspect that's age talking and not character.",null],["At twenty I'd have given you a different answer with great confidence.",null],["Having said that, I notice my daughters at university have thirty friends each and seem no less attached to any of them, so I may simply be describing my own limits.","family"]],[["Is it easy to make friends in your city?","friends"]],[["Easy to meet people, harder to make friends, and I'd say what's short is time, not goodwill.","friends"],["Friends here used to be made by living near each other.","friends"],["The same street in Damascus, the same school, forty years.","map"],["Nobody's got that any more, so it has to be done on purpose, and most adults aren't very good at doing anything on purpose.",null]],[["Have your friendships changed over the years?",null]],[["The ones that lasted became much less about anything.",null],["When we were young we were friends over football, over politics, over a hundred things.","friends"],["Now we're friends in Damascus over having been friends, which sounds thin and it's in fact the only kind that survives.","friends"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about letters and messages.","letter"]],[["Do you often write letters or emails?","letter"]],[["Emails constantly, letters never, and I notice that I mourn the second more than it deserves.","letter"],["You see, I've got about four letters from my father and nothing at all from anyone who's still alive.","letter"]],[["Do you prefer to phone people or to message them?","phone"]],[["Message, which I'd have found unthinkable at thirty.","phone"],["A message lets the other person answer when they're ready, and, you see, let me put it this way.","phone"],["Most of what I say isn't urgent enough to interrupt a man's afternoon in Damascus.","map"]],[["Have you kept any letters you received?","letter"]],[["Four, in a drawer in Damascus, and I've read none of them for about a decade.","map"],["Having said that, I know precisely where they are, which probably suggests the reading isn't the point.",null]],[["Will people still write letters in the future?","letter"]],[["As something you do, I'd say, and not as something you use.",null],["The practical job has gone elsewhere permanently.","office"],["What survives will be the part that was never about delivering information in the first place, the way a card at a wedding in Damascus isn't really a card.","letter"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t06p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a person who influenced you.",null]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I want to talk about a man called Ustaz Fouad, who taught me Arabic at my school in Damascus when I was sixteen and who I then worked alongside for eleven years, which is a strange thing to happen to anybody.","school"]],[["He was not, I should say, a warm man.",null]],[["He never raised his voice in thirty years and he never told anyone they'd done well either, and both of those facts were known to every student in the building.","stairs"]],[["You did not go to Ustaz Fouad for encouragement.",null]],[["What he did was wait.",null],["You'd answer a question badly and he would simply not say anything.",null],["Not in a cold way.","winter"]],[["He'd just leave the silence there, and after about four seconds you'd start talking again, and the second thing you said was always better than the first.",null]],[["Every single time.",null],["I watched him do it to four hundred children and I have never seen it fail.",null]],[["The influence is that I've spent my entire career trying to do that and finding it almost impossible.",null],["It looks like doing nothing.",null]],[["It is in fact the hardest thing in a classroom, because everything you feel as a teacher, and everything you feel as a kind person, tells you to rescue a child who is struggling in front of thirty others.","school"]],[["He understood something I'm still only half able to act on, which is that rescuing them is for you and not for them.",null]],[["He died in 2019.",null],["I teach about a third as well as he did, and I'd say that's the single most useful thing I know about myself.","school"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you think he knew he had that effect on you?",null]],[["I never told him, which I regret in a mild, ordinary way.",null],["Mind you, knowing him, he'd have found a way not to respond to it.",null],["Probably he'd have waited, of course, until I'd said something better, and everyone who taught in Damascus with him would tell you the same.","map"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t06p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a person who influenced you, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.",null]],[["Let's consider families and generations.","family"]],[["Do children today have a different relationship with their parents?","family"]],[["Different in kind, I'd say, and I don't think closer or further is the right comparison.",null],["My father and I had enormous respect and almost no conversation.","family"],["My daughters and I talk constantly and they'd argue with me about anything.","family"],["I wouldn't claim one of those is obviously better.",null],["They aren't the same thing, because you can't measure them on one scale.",null]],[["What can older generations learn from younger ones?","generations"]],[["How quickly a thing can be abandoned, and I mean that kindly.",null],["The men I grew up with finished things.",null],["We kept jobs we disliked for decades because leaving looked like giving up.",null],["The young people I teach in Damascus will walk away from something that isn't working in a fortnight, and watching them do it, I'm no longer sure which of us was being sensible.","map"]],[["Some people say families are less important than they used to be.","family"],["Would you agree?",null]],[["I'd agree they're less obligatory, and then I'd want to argue about what that means.",null],["For a very long time in Syria the family was the only thing that worked.","map"],["Your insurance, your work, your old age.",null],["Those jobs have mostly gone elsewhere.",null],["Having said that, what's left when you take the jobs away is the part people actually valued, so I'd be careful calling that a decline.",null]],[["Now let's talk about who children learn from.",null]],[["Who has the greatest influence on children — parents, teachers or friends?","family"]],[["Parents for who they are, friends for what they do, teachers for almost nothing, and I say that, of course, as a teacher of thirty years.","family"],["Take my own case.",null],["I've described a man who changed my working life, and he got about four hours a week of me for two years.","calendar"],["My father got everything else.","family"],["The honest ratio isn't flattering to my profession.",null]],[["Will teachers still be important in the future?",null]],[["Less as somewhere to get information and more as somewhere to be noticed, I'd imagine.",null],["The one thing I can do that nothing else currently can, I think, is see that a child in a Damascus classroom has gone quiet in the third week of October.","calendar"],["I wouldn't be surprised if that becomes the whole job, and I don't think that's a smaller version of it.","office"]],[["Should schools teach values as well as subjects?","school"]],[["They do, whether anyone intends it or not, and that's the part of this question I'd quarrel with.",null],["A school in Damascus that teaches nothing but chemistry still teaches that you queue, that you wait to be asked, that adults are believed before children.","map"],["The real question isn't whether to teach values.","school"],["It's whether to be honest about which ones you're already teaching.","school"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t07m.js":[[["The same fault as Test 4, eleven weeks later, now costing a full band instead of half of one.","calendar"]],[["The long turn is well shaped and the deferred feeling — the anticlimax, then the real moment three weeks later — is a genuinely sophisticated structure.","calendar"],["She still signposts well.",null],["What dropped half a band from Test 4 is control of her own sentence length: five times she extends a sentence that had already landed, and twice the extension loses the thread of the clause it started from.","exam"],["The content is band 8.",null],["The management of it is not.",null]],[["Unchanged from Test 4 and the strongest thing about this performance.","exam"],["Both quotations are constructed rather than retrieved, and the second is a metaphor she builds mid-answer and does not over-explain.",null],["Idiom is natural and the style-awareness is real — she drops register deliberately ('I'll be honest', 'so.') and raises it when she is arguing.",null],["This criterion is carrying her overall band.",null]],[["This is where the half band went, and it is the same fault as Test 4 — worse.","exam"],["Two outright concord errors survive uncorrected, both in the accelerated final third of a long sentence, and both of a kind she does not make when speaking slowly.",null],["In Test 4 the same habit produced five slips that mostly did not obscure meaning; here two of them do make a listener re-run the clause.","exam"],["Attempted complexity with errors that cause comprehension problems is a band 6 description.",null],["Her range is not the issue and never has been.",null]],[["Clear, lively, and still losing the ends of long sentences exactly as she did eleven weeks ago.","calendar"],["The three-beat passage is excellent and shows she can control pace when the sentence is short.",null],["Nothing has deteriorated and nothing has improved.",null]],[["The advice after Test 4 was: slow the last five words of every long sentence.","exam"],["She did not, and the cost has gone up — the same habit that took half a band off Grammar in Test 4 has now taken a full band off it, because two of the errors are no longer invisible.","money"],["This is the most useful thing a reader can take from comparing Tests 4 and 7: UNFIXED HABITS DO NOT STAY THE SAME SIZE.","exam"],["The fix has not changed.",null],["Finish the sentence at the speed you started it.",null]]],"t07p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Rawan Saleh.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Rawan is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Rawan.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about shopping.","souk"]],[["Do you enjoy shopping?","souk"]],[["For some things, honestly.",null],["Clothes, no.","clothes"],["I find it exhausting and I'll always buy the same thing in a different colour.","colours"],["But a market in Damascus, for food, I could do that for hours and I wouldn't get bored.","map"]],[["Do you prefer shopping online or in shops?","souk"]],[["Online for anything I already know, shops for anything I don't.","laptop"],["The thing is, half of what I'd buy online doesn't ship to Syria anyway, so it isn't always a real choice.","laptop"],["You end up with whoever a friend knows who's travelling.","friends"]],[["Has the way you shop changed in recent years?","souk"]],[["I buy much less, and not for a noble reason.",null],["I just got tired of owning things.","heart_health"],["Actually that's not quite true.",null],["I got tired of moving things, which is different, and I've moved flat four times in six years in Damascus.","heart_health"]],[["Do you think people buy too much?",null]],[["Probably, although I'm not sure I'm allowed an opinion when the reason I buy less is that things in Syria are expensive.","map"],["I'd like to say it's a principle.",null],["People with money buy things.","money"],["That isn't a moral failing.",null],["It's just what money does.","money"]],[["Let's move on to talk about saving and spending.",null]],[["Do you find it easy to save money?","money"]],[["No, and willpower isn't the problem.",null],["When you're paid in one currency and live in Damascus, saving is a decision about which one to be wrong in.","money"],["I've basically given up on being clever about it.",null]],[["Did your parents teach you about money?","family"]],[["My father did, in a very specific way.","family"],["He'd give me the whole month's amount on the first day and then not rescue me.",null],["Which was harsh and also the only reason I can run a business in Damascus now, so.","map"]],[["What do you usually spend your money on?","money"]],[["Rent, obviously, and then the two things I won't economise on, which are, I suppose, good coffee and the internet.","cafe"],["Everyone I know in Damascus has their two.","map"],["Nobody's are the same and everybody defends theirs completely seriously.",null]],[["Is it important to save for the future?",null]],[["It is, and I'd say it's also a luxury to be able to think that far ahead.",null],["Save for the future assumes you know roughly what the future costs.","money"],["A lot of people in Damascus are budgeting for about three weeks, and that isn't them being careless.","map"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about clothes.","clothes"]],[["Are clothes important to you?","clothes"]],[["Less than they were, honestly.",null],["When I started working in Damascus I thought I needed a version of myself that looked professional.","map"],["Then I realised nobody I work for has ever seen me below the shoulders, so it doesn't matter and I don't bother.",null]],[["Do you prefer comfortable clothes or fashionable ones?","clothes"]],[["Comfortable, and I'd have lied about that at twenty-two.",null],["The thing is, when you work at home in a Damascus flat the only person judging what you're wearing is you, and it turns out I'm not very strict.","home"]],[["Do you buy clothes often?","clothes"]],[["Rarely, and badly.",null],["I buy the same thing in a different colour, which I think I've told you already, so clearly it's bothering me more than I let on.","colours"],["There's one shop near me in Damascus and that's where I go.","souk"]],[["Do people in your country dress differently now from in the past?","clothes"]],[["Yes and no, right?",null],["The street looks different, obviously.","street"],["But a wedding or an Eid morning looks, I think, almost exactly like my mother's photographs.","mosque"],["The occasions kept their clothes even when the ordinary days didn't.","clothes"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t07p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe something you saved money for.","money"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["Everyone expects me to say the laptop, and the laptop was a work expense, so honestly it doesn't count.","laptop"],["The thing I actually saved for was a chair.","chair"],["An office chair.","chair"]],[["Which sounds silly until you've sat on a kitchen stool for nine hours a day for two years.","chair"]],[["I was twenty-six and my back was the back of a much older person, and I'd been told by everybody, by my mother, by a doctor, by the internet, and I ignored all of them, because wanting a chair felt stupid.","chair"]],[["It took seven months.","calendar"],["The way I did it was daft, I'll admit.",null]],[["I cut out coffee, which is the one thing I've just told you I don't economise on, and I was unhappy for seven months in order to be comfortable afterwards.","cafe"]],[["If I was advising someone I'd tell them to take fewer jobs and raise their prices instead.",null],["I didn't have the nerve at that age.",null]],[["And when it arrived, this is the part I didn't expect, I felt almost nothing.",null],["Nothing at all.",null],["It's a chair.","chair"],["You sit on it.","chair"],["There's no moment.",null]],[["The feeling came about three weeks later, when I'd worked a whole day and then stood up without thinking about it.","calendar"]],[["That's when I nearly cried, over an office chair, which I'd like it noted is a perfectly reasonable response to seven months of not drinking coffee.","cafe"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Have you saved for anything since?",null]],[["Nothing that big.",null],["I've got better at just buying the boring thing that fixes the problem, which I think is what being twenty-seven is.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t07p3.js":[[["We've been talking about something you saved money for, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","money"]],[["Let's consider owning things.",null]],[["Do people today own more possessions than in the past?",null]],[["Far more, although I'd point out that most of it is cheap and none of it lasts, so 'own' is doing something odd in that sentence.","money"],["My grandmother had maybe thirty objects in her whole kitchen and every one of them outlived her.","family"],["I have more things and I'd guess a smaller total weight of actual stuff.",null]],[["Does owning more things make people happier?",null]],[["Depends entirely which things.",null],["There's a point where it obviously does.",null],["A chair, a washing machine, anything that takes a daily problem away.","chair"],["Past that it doesn't, and I think everyone in Damascus secretly knows where their own line is and crosses it anyway.","map"]],[["Are young people today less interested in owning homes?",null]],[["Less able, I'd say, and then it becomes less interested afterwards, which is how most preferences work.",null],["Nobody I know in Damascus has seriously considered buying a flat, and they don't talk about it either.","map"],["When a thing is that far out of reach people stops wanting it, which is a kind of protection really.",null]],[["Now let's talk about money and young people.","money"]],[["Should schools teach children about money?","school"]],[["Yes, and specifically the boring parts.",null],["Not investment, which is what people always mean.",null],["It's things like understanding what a commission actually costs you.","money"],["I didn't learn that at university in Damascus.","map"],["I learned it by losing about an eighth of a payment and spending a day working out why.",null]],[["Is it easier or harder for young people to manage money now?","money"]],[["Harder, although I'd admit it's also much easier to see.",null],["My father had no idea what he spent.","family"],["We can all see every transfer now, which helps, and of course it also makes it worse, because there's no comfortable fog to hide in any more.",null]],[["Will people use physical money in the future?","money"]],[["Less, obviously, though I think I'd be careful predicting it disappears.",null],["Cash works when nothing else does.",null],["No power, no network, no bank.","solar"],["Anywhere that's happened, and it's happened in Damascus, people keep some.","map"],["I wouldn't be surprised if cash survive for a very long time as the thing you fall back on.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t08m.js":[[["The most accurate English of the two performances below band 7, and the lowest vocabulary mark — because she never risks a word she might miss.",null]],[["She is willing and she is never incoherent — a listener follows her without any effort at all.",null],["But she cannot sustain.",null],["Eleven of her fourteen short answers are two sentences or fewer, and six of them end by restating their own first sentence.",null],["The long turn runs out at about a minute and thirty-five with the final bullet barely addressed.","clock"],["Her connectives are 'and also' and 'because', used sixteen times between them and almost nothing else.",null],["Repeating the question back — which she does three times — is a stalling device the examiner hears immediately.","exam"],["One consequence is worth every reader's attention: because she keeps finishing early, SHE IS ASKED MORE QUESTIONS — twelve in Part 3 where most candidates in this book are asked six.","clock"],["The part is four to five minutes whether you fill it or not.","clock"],["Answering short does not shorten the test; it buys you twice as many chances to go wrong.","exam"]],[["Adequate for every topic she was given and precise on none of them.",null],["The feeling words are the clearest evidence: across the whole test she evaluates everything with 'nice', 'amazing', 'good' and 'peaceful' and never once reaches for anything more exact.","exam"],["There is no paraphrase anywhere, because she never attempts a word she might miss.",null],["The exception is quoted above and it matters — 'small, in a good way' is her working out a meaning in real time, and it is the only moment in eleven minutes where she does.","clock"],["One attempt is not a range.",null]],[["HER HIGHEST MARK, and candidates should look hard at that.",null],["She makes very few errors — two articles, one double subject, nothing else in eleven minutes — and she handles past, present, conditional and a contrastive 'but' clause cleanly.","clock"],["What she does not do is reach: the complex sentences are few, and they are all built the same way.",null],["A range of structures used with flexibility is the band 7 description and she just about meets it.",null],["She is accurate well beyond her other three criteria, and that accuracy is earning her nothing, because she is spending it on sentences of nine words.",null]],[["Easy to understand from start to finish, with occasional real expressiveness when she is interested in what she is saying — the line about the stars is well delivered.","night"],["L1 features are present and cost nothing.","gift"],["The ceiling here is simply that short, simple sentences give her few chances to show the features of connected speech that a band 8 needs; you cannot demonstrate rhythm across a clause you did not say.",null]],[["Nour has to be told something that sounds like a contradiction: her English is good enough for band 7 and her ANSWERS are not.",null],["The single change is the third beat.",null],["She reliably gives an answer and a reason, and almost never the specific — and in the two answers where a specific appears ('my uncle's house in the mountains', 'my father put it there two years ago', the argument about the roof) she immediately becomes interesting and the English gets better, not worse.","home"],["One concrete detail per answer would raise Fluency and Lexical Resource together, because a specific forces vocabulary a generality never needs.",null],["That is 7.0, and it requires her to learn nothing.",null]]],"t08p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Nour Khalil.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Nour is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Nour.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about weather.","winter"]],[["Do you like the weather where you live?","winter"]],[["Do I like the weather?","winter"],["Yes, I like it.",null],["It is usually sunny and I prefer sunny weather.","summer"]],[["Is there any kind of weather you don't like?","winter"]],[["I don't like the cold.","winter"],["In winter it is very cold and I don't like it.","winter"]],[["Does the weather affect what you do?","winter"]],[["Yes, sometimes.",null],["If it is raining I stay at home and also I don't go out with my friends.","home"]],[["Do you prefer summer or winter?","summer"]],[["Summer, I think, because in summer we have holiday and I can see my friends, and also we go to my uncle's house in the mountains.","summer"],["It's nice.",null]],[["Let's move on to talk about being outdoors.",null]],[["Do you spend much time outdoors?",null]],[["Not very much.",null],["Because I am studying, so I am inside most of the time.",null]],[["What do you like to do outdoors?",null]],[["I like walking.",null],["Me and my friends, we walk in the evening sometimes, near the university, and we talk and we've got coffee there.","friends"],["It's nice.",null],["Something like that, I think.",null]],[["Did you spend more time outdoors as a child?",null]],[["Yes, much more.",null],["When I was a child we played in the street every day after school.","school"],["Now it is different.",null]],[["Is it important for children to play outside?",null]],[["Yes, it's important for the health and also they learn how to be with other children.",null],["It's important.",null]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about colours.","colours"]],[["What's your favourite colour?","colours"]],[["My favourite colour is green.","colours"],["I like green because it is a nice colour and also it is the colour of nature.","colours"]],[["Do you like wearing bright colours?","clothes"]],[["Not very much.",null],["I usually wear dark colours, like black or grey.","colours"],["I don't know why, it's just what I buy.",null]],[["Are there any colours you don't like?","colours"]],[["Orange.","colours"],["I don't like orange at all.","colours"],["My sister painted her room in our building orange and it was really bad, everybody said so, and she's painted it again after two months.","colours"]],[["Do you think colours can affect how people feel?","colours"]],[["Yes, I think so.",null],["For example in the hospital the colours are very light and it's calm.","hospital"],["And also in a restaurant they use warm colours, because they don't want you to leave.","colours"],["So yes, I think it affects people.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t08p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a time when you really noticed nature.","stars_sky"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I will talk about when I was at my uncle's house, in the mountains near Zabadani.","mountains"],["It was in the summer, last year I think, or two years ago.","summer"]],[["I was with my family.","family"],["My parents and my brother and also my cousins were there.","family"],["It's a big house and everybody comes in the summer.","home"]],[["What I noticed was the stars.","night"],["In Damascus you cannot see the stars because there is too much light, but there, at night, there were so many.","night"]],[["I was sitting outside and I looked up and it was really amazing.","chair"]],[["My brother was there also and we stayed outside for a long time.","family"]],[["It made me feel very peaceful.",null],["And also small, in a good way, because you see how big it is.",null],["It was a really nice feeling and I remember it.",null]],[["Yes.",null],["So that was the time I noticed nature.","stars_sky"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you often go to the mountains?","mountains"]],[["Only in summer.","summer"],["Maybe two or three times.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t08p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a time when you really noticed nature, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","stars_sky"]],[["Let's consider cities and green space.","map"]],[["Do cities in your country have enough green space?","map"]],[["No, not enough.",null],["There are some parks but not many, and they are crowded.","queue"],["I think we need more parks.",null]],[["Why do you think green spaces are important in cities?","park"]],[["Because people need to relax and also it's good for the air.",null],["If there are trees the air is better.","park"],["And children in Damascus need somewhere to play, and they haven't got anywhere, I think.","map"]],[["Do you think cities will have more green space in the future?","map"]],[["I hope so.",null],["Maybe, if the government builds more parks.",null],["But I don't know really.",null],["I think maybe yes.",null]],[["Who should pay for parks — the government or local people?","money"]],[["The government, I think.",null],["Because it's for everybody, so everybody should...",null],["yes, the government.",null]],[["Are there other ways to make a city greener, apart from parks?","map"]],[["Trees in the street.","park"],["And also on the balconies.",null],["A lot of people have plants on the balcony here.","balcony"],["It's nice.",null]],[["Is that common where you live?",null]],[["Yes, very common, I think.",null],["In our building almost every balcony has something.","stairs"],["My mother has about fifteen pots and she's talking to them, which my brother doesn't understand at all.","family"]],[["Now let's talk about energy at home.","home"]],[["How do people in your country try to save energy at home?","map"]],[["We are careful with the electricity.","solar"],["Like, you don't leave the lights on, and we've got a solar panel on the roof because my father put it there two years ago.","solar"],["Everybody in our building has one now, and in the beginning, well, they were arguing about the space on the roof.","stairs"]],[["Should governments do more to encourage people to save energy?","solar"]],[["Yes, I think they should do more.",null],["Maybe they can make it cheaper for people to buy the solar panels.","solar"],["That would help.",null]],[["Will people use different kinds of energy in the future?","solar"]],[["Yes, I think so.",null],["More solar, probably, because there is a lot of sun here.","solar"],["It's better for the environment also.",null]],[["Do you think people are more careful with energy than they used to be?","solar"]],[["Yes, definitely more careful.",null],["Before, nobody was thinking about it.","thinking"],["Now everybody is thinking about it.","thinking"]],[["Why do you think that changed?",null]],[["Because of the price, mostly.","money"],["When something is expensive you are careful with it.","money"],["That's normal I think.",null]],[["Can you give me an example of something your family does differently now?","family"]],[["We do the washing in the morning now, not at night.","night"],["Because my father says the machine is better in the morning.","family"],["I don't know if that's true actually, but we all do it.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t09m.js":[[["A band lower than Test 6 with no loss of English at all — the topic moved one step off his ground and took a band with it.","exam"]],[["Unchanged from Test 6 and still the best in the book alongside Rawan's Test 4.","exam"],["The long turn is built on a deferred realisation and the title he cannot remember is turned into evidence for his own argument rather than treated as a failure — that is control.",null],["He recovers from the one genuine gap (film vocabulary) without losing the thread, which is precisely the band 8 description of occasional hesitation to find content rather than language.","television"]],[["A band lower than Test 6, and for exactly the reason Hadi dropped in Test 5: the resource is magnificent on his own ground and measurably thinner one step off it.","exam"],["On books, teaching, students and time he is a band 9 speaker.","library"],["On film he twice flags a gap himself and once settles for a general word where a precise one was wanted.","television"],["Flagging it honestly is the right strategy and saved the answers; it does not restore the mark.",null],["This is the most useful lesson in his two tests: range is not a single number you own, it is topic-shaped.","exam"]],[["A band below Test 6.","exam"],["The range is unchanged and remains wide, but there is one clear concord error left uncorrected, a second slip in the Part 1 answer about films, and three self-corrections mid-clause where in Test 6 there were none.","exam"],["Frequent error-free sentences rather than a majority of them.",null],["It is worth noticing where the error sits: in the one Part 3 answer where he was reaching for an idea he had not had before.",null],["His grammar breaks in the same place everyone's does — under the load of new thought.",null]],[["Identical to Test 6 and identically costly.","exam"],["The same category of word — long, Latinate, abstract — takes the same misplaced stress, six times here.",null],["Everything else about his delivery is band 8 or above.",null],["Three months and one band of overall movement later, this fault has not shifted at all, which is what an unaddressed pronunciation habit does: nothing, forever, until it is specifically worked on.","calendar"]],[["The same six words as Test 6, and he has not touched them.","exam"],["But the new lesson here is about RANGE: Samir lost a band between two tests without losing any English, because one test was about his subject and the other was half a step outside it.","exam"],["Readers who are confident on their own topics should take this seriously — the fix is not more vocabulary in general, it is deliberately preparing the three or four common IELTS topics furthest from your own life.",null],["For him that is film, sport and technology.","gym"]]],"t09p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Samir Othman.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Samir is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Samir.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about news and reading.","television"]],[["How do you usually follow the news?","television"]],[["Badly, and deliberately so.",null],["I read one long thing at the weekend and I've given up on the daily version entirely.","calendar"],["You see, I found I was better informed and worse at thinking, which seemed a poor trade.","thinking"],["That's what thirty years in a Damascus classroom does to you.","map"]],[["Do you read newspapers?","newspaper"]],[["On paper, no, not for years.","notebook"],["I miss the shape of it, though.",null],["A paper had edges, you finished it.","notebook"],["Nothing finishes now.",null]],[["Do you prefer reading books or watching television?","library"]],[["Books, overwhelmingly, and honestly I'd better admit that's partly showing off.","library"],["I teach in Damascus and I'm supposed to say that.","map"],["But the honest reason is slower: a book waits for you and a programme doesn't.","television"]],[["What kind of books do you enjoy?","library"]],[["Poetry, mostly, which makes me insufferable at parties in Damascus.","book"],["And I reread more than I read.",null],["I've probably got forty books I've been through four times and a hundred I've never opened.","library"]],[["Let's move on to talk about films.","television"]],[["Do you watch many films?","television"]],[["Not many, no.",null],["My daughters watch a great deal and I usually end up in the room, because our building is small.","family"],["So I've seen a lot of films the way you've been to a place you were driven through.","television"]],[["What kind of films do you like?","television"]],[["The slow ones, I suppose.",null],["The ones where, although I don't know the proper words for this, I'm afraid, because I teach books in Damascus and not films.","library"],["The kind where very little happens and you watch people's faces.",null]],[["Do you prefer watching films at home or at the cinema?","television"]],[["At home, now, I suppose, though that's circumstances and not choice.","home"],["The cinema has an attention you can't get on a sofa, everybody facing one way in the dark.","television"],["That's quite a hard thing to arrange in Damascus and we've stopped trying.","map"]],[["Are films a good way to learn about other countries?","television"]],[["They're a good way to learn what a country wants to be seen as, which isn't the same thing and is possibly more interesting.","map"],["Having said that, I've learned more about English life from one television series than from twenty years of teaching its books in Damascus, so I should be careful.","television"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about mornings.",null]],[["Are you a morning person?",null]],[["I've become one, which I'm told happens to everybody and I didn't enjoy being told.",null],["For about twenty years I did my marking at night, well, after the school in Damascus closed.","map"],["Now I do it at five, and it's better work, which is annoying.",null]],[["What do you usually do first in the morning?",null]],[["Coffee, and about twenty minutes of doing nothing at all, which my wife thinks is a waste and which is, I think, the only genuinely free part of my day.","cafe"],["There isn't another one that's mine in Damascus.","map"]],[["Do you think breakfast is an important meal?","meal"]],[["Important is doing some work in that question.",null],["It's the meal I'd miss most, which isn't the same as needing it.","meal"],["You see, I'm quite capable of teaching all morning in Damascus without it and completely incapable of enjoying the morning.","school"]],[["Do children in your country start school early?","clock"]],[["Early enough that nobody has had breakfast properly, which every teacher in Damascus will tell you and nothing is done about.","clock"],["I've taught the first lesson of the day for thirty years, and honestly it isn't the lesson anyone learns anything in.","school"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t09p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a film or a book that made you think.","television"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I'm going to do something slightly against type and talk about a film, partly because it would be too easy for me to talk about a book.","television"]],[["My daughters put it on last winter, here in Damascus.","family"]],[["A long film, very slow, from somewhere in eastern Europe, and I have to admit I don't remember the title correctly, and that tells you something about what follows.","television"]],[["I sat down for ten minutes out of politeness and watched the whole thing.","chair"],["What it's about, in the sense of plot, is almost nothing.",null],["A man travels somewhere.",null]],[["There's a great deal of water and long silences and a scene with a candle that goes on for, I want to say nine minutes, and my daughters insist it's four, so somewhere between the two.","water"]],[["Why it made me think was not the film's subject at all.",null]],[["It was that I was bored for the first forty minutes, genuinely bored, and then at some point I stopped being bored without anything having changed on the screen.","clock"]],[["And I've thought about that a great deal since, because I spend my working life telling teenagers that a poem is slow on purpose and that they should stay with it, and I had never once tested whether I could do it myself with something I wasn't already trained to like.","book"]],[["I'd always assumed being bored was a verdict.",null],["It turns out it may just be a stage you have to go through, and I'd been teaching that for thirty years without having the evidence.","school"]],[["Thank you.",null],["Have you watched it again?",null]],[["No, and I've noticed I keep not doing it, which is probably cowardice.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t09p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a film or a book that made you think, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","television"]],[["Let's consider where people get news.","television"]],[["How has the way people get news changed?","television"]],[["The direction of it changed, I'd say.",null],["In Damascus news used to arrive, at a time, in a form, whether you wanted it or not, and now it's something you go and fetch, which sounds like more freedom and may well be less.","map"],["If you're fetching it, you fetch what you already wanted.",null]],[["Some people say there is too much news now.","television"],["Would you agree?",null]],[["Too much news, or too much of the same news repeated?","television"],["I'd say the second.",null],["Having said that, every generation in Damascus has said this, usually about whatever arrived after they turned forty, so I don't hold the opinion very tightly.","generations"]],[["Will newspapers still exist in twenty years?","newspaper"]],[["In some form, though probably as a luxury and not as a tool, the way a printed book in Damascus has become.","map"],["I wouldn't be surprised if the thing that survives is the editing and not the paper.","notebook"],["Somebody choosing what matters is a service, and we've temporarily forgotten we were paying for it.","bus"]],[["Now let's talk about stories and real life.",null]],[["Can people learn about real life from stories?",null]],[["They can learn what it feels like, which isn't the same as learning what it is, and I think the confusion between those two causes quite a lot of trouble.",null],["A story gives you the inside of an experience you haven't had.",null],["It doesn't give you how often it happens, and my students in Damascus constantly mistake a vivid story for a common one.","map"]],[["Why do people enjoy sad stories?",null]],[["Because it's grief you can put down.",null],["That's the whole of it, I think.",null],["Take my students in Damascus.","map"],["The ones who cry over a poem about a dead father are mostly not the ones with dead fathers.","book"],["It's a rehearsal, and you need somewhere to rehearse.",null]],[["Do you think films and books will be replaced by something new?","library"]],[["Something will come, obviously, and I'd guess the mistake people makes is to assume the new form replaces the old one.",null],["It usually doesn't.",null],["Photography was supposed to end painting and what it actually did was free it.",null],["Nobody in Damascus stopped painting.","map"],["So I'd expect one more thing, not one thing fewer, if I had to put money on it.","money"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]],"t10m.js":[[["The best-prepared candidate here, held at 7.0 by the preparation itself.","exam"]],[["He speaks at length comfortably and the long turn is genuinely well structured — the ceremony dismissed in three sentences so that the courtyard can have ninety.",null],["The repair in the second quotation is excellent and should be studied: he deploys a memorised idiom, hears that it does not fit, says so, and supplies the plain version without losing a beat.",null],["That is band 7 self-correction.",null],["What holds him there rather than higher is the first quotation — the learned frame stalls him mid-sentence and he has to start the clause twice.",null]],[["There are two vocabularies in this transcript and they belong to different candidates.",null],["One builds 'a square of light that was about to disappear' and 'a date everybody has already agreed to', which is band 8 work.",null],["The other deploys fourteen instances of four memorised frames, one of them in a context where it means nothing at all.",null],["The frames are not errors and they are not penalised as errors — but they occupy the opening of his answers, which is where range is most visible, and they make a genuinely flexible resource sound rehearsed.",null],["The band is a straight average of the two.",null]],[["A real range used flexibly: narrative past perfect, both common conditionals, relative clauses, a passive used because it was the right choice.",null],["Frequent error-free sentences and very few slips — two articles across the test.","exam"],["He does not reach 8 because the sentences that open with a learned frame are noticeably simpler than the ones that do not; the scaffolding is holding his syntax down as well as his vocabulary.",null]],[["Clear and easy to follow, with good use of stress when the words are his.",null],["The distinctive feature — and it is unusual enough to be worth naming — is that an examiner can HEAR the learned phrases: they come out faster, flatter and with an intonation contour that does not match the sentence around them.","exam"],["That is a real pronunciation observation and not a metaphor.",null],["It makes the rehearsal audible, which is the opposite of what the phrases were meant to achieve.",null]],[["Delete four phrases.",null],["'In my opinion', 'I would say that', 'to a certain extent' and 'as far as I'm concerned' appear fourteen times in this test and contribute nothing to a single answer.","exam"],["Every time Omar does without them he produces band 8 material — the envelope with its unwritten rate, the stranger who took the photograph, the square of light.","letter"],["His problem is not a lack of preparation; it is the preparation.",null],["Say the thing directly and the other three criteria rise with the vocabulary, because the simplified syntax and the flat delivery are attached to the same four phrases.",null]]],"t10p1.js":[[["Good morning.",null],["Come in, have a seat.","chair"]],[["My name is Mohamad Khorbatli.",null],["Can you tell me your full name, please?",null]],[["Omar Daher.",null]],[["And what can I call you?",null]],[["Omar is fine.",null]],[["Thank you, Omar.",null],["Can I see your identification, please?",null]],[["Of course.",null],["Here you are.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That's all fine.",null]],[["Now, in this first part I'd like to ask you some questions about yourself.",null],["Let's talk about celebrations.","mosque"]],[["What celebrations are important in your country?","mosque"]],[["Well, as far as I'm concerned, the most important celebration is Eid.","mosque"],["Both of them, but especially the one after Ramadan.","mosque"],["The whole country stops for about three days and you visit everybody, in a fixed order, which everyone pretends isn't a rule and absolutely is not.","map"]],[["How do you usually celebrate?",null]],[["At my uncle's, which is where everyone goes.",null],["I would say that the main feature is food, obviously, but actually the thing I like is the morning.","meal"],["You're out at about seven to pray and the streets are full of people in new clothes who are all slightly embarrassed.","mosque"]],[["Do you give presents on these occasions?","gift"]],[["To the children, yes, of course.",null],["Money, in an envelope, and there's a rate in Damascus that nobody has ever written down and everybody knows it.","letter"],["If you've got it wrong you hear about it for a year.",null]],[["Have celebrations changed since you were a child?","mosque"]],[["To a certain extent, yes.",null],["The structure is the same.",null],["What's changed is the number of people in the room, because a lot of my cousins have left Damascus now, so there's a long section of the day that's spent on video calls, with the phone propped against the fruit bowl.","family"]],[["Let's move on to talk about music.","music"]],[["Do you listen to music often?","music"]],[["Quite often, in my opinion.",null],["Well, I mean, yes, every day, mostly walking to work in Damascus.","map"]],[["What kind of music do you like?","music"]],[["Older Arabic music, mostly, I suppose, because it's the long ones, forty minutes, where the singer repeats one line about nine times and each time it's different.","music"],["My friends in Damascus find this unbearable.","friends"]],[["Has your taste in music changed?","music"]],[["It has, yes.",null],["When I was younger I listened to whatever was popular and I would say that I didn't really choose it.",null],["Now I choose, and I'm choosing things my father listened to, which, honestly, I find slightly annoying about myself.","family"]],[["Do you think music is important for young people?","music"]],[["Very important, in my opinion.",null],["Well, it's a way to express themselves and also to find people who are similar to them.",null],["When I was at university in Damascus you could tell which group somebody belonged to in about four seconds, and it wasn't from anything except the music.","map"]],[["Finally in this part, let's talk about being on time.",null]],[["Are you usually on time?",null]],[["I would say that I am, yes.",null],["Well, I'm early, which is a different problem.","clock"],["I'll arrive at the university about fifteen minutes before everything and then stand somewhere pretending to look at my phone.","clock"]],[["Do you think being late is rude?","clock"]],[["To a certain extent, I suppose.",null],["It depends very much on the occasion, because nobody's early to a family lunch in Damascus and nobody'd want you to be.","clock"],["But a job interview, obviously.","microphone"],["The same ten minutes means two completely different things.","clock"]],[["How do you usually plan your day?",null]],[["Badly, in my opinion.",null],["I write a list every morning before I leave for work in Damascus and I've never once finished one.","map"],["At this point, honestly, the list only records what I'd meant to do, which is a different thing from a plan.",null]],[["Is being punctual important in your culture?","clock"]],[["Less than in some, and I'd be careful about how that gets described.",null],["There's a sort of agreed flexibility.",null],["An invitation for eight in Damascus means somewhere after half past, and everyone knows it, so nobody is actually late.","map"],["It's a different clock, and not a worse one.","clock"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part One.",null]]],"t10p2.js":[[["Now, I'm going to give you a topic and I'd like you to talk about it for one to two minutes.","clock"]],[["Before you talk you'll have one minute to think about what you're going to say.","clock"],["You can make some notes if you wish.","notebook"],["Do you understand?",null]],[["Yes, I understand.",null]],[["Here is some paper and a pencil for making notes, and here is your topic.","notebook"]],[["I'd like you to describe a celebration you took part in.","mosque"]],[["You have one minute.","clock"],["I'll tell you when the minute is up.","clock"]],[["All right?",null],["Remember you have one to two minutes for this.","clock"],["Don't worry if I stop you.",null],["I'll tell you when the time is up.",null],["Can you start speaking now, please?",null]],[["I would like to describe the day I finished at the university here in Damascus, which was about three years ago.","map"]],[["The ceremony itself was, to be honest, quite long and not very interesting.",null],["We sat in a hall for two hours and names were read out.","chair"]],[["My mother cried, which she had promised not to do.","family"]],[["But the part I want to describe happened afterwards, outside, in the square in the middle of the buildings.","stairs"]],[["There was a photographer and there were about two hundred families all trying to take the same photograph in the same forty minutes, because that's how long the light lasts in there.","family"]],[["It has buildings on four sides and the sun goes off it very suddenly.","stairs"]],[["So what you had was two hundred families being extremely polite to each other and extremely competitive at the same time.","family"],["People were holding other people's flowers.","balcony"]],[["A man I had never met organised my entire family into a row and then took the photograph as well, and we have never seen him again.","family"]],[["Why it was memorable.",null],["I would say that it was memorable because, well, because it wasn't about me.",null]],[["For five years the whole thing had been about my marks, my exams, whether I would finish.","exam"]],[["And then the actual day was two hundred families doing the same thing at once in a square of light that was about to disappear, and I was one of them.","family"]],[["That was better, somehow.",null],["Smaller and better.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["Do you still have that photograph?","photograph"]],[["It's in my mother's living room, enlarged to an unreasonable size.",null],["The stranger did a very good job.","office"]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of Part Two.",null]]],"t10p3.js":[[["We've been talking about a celebration you took part in, and I'd like to discuss with you one or two more general questions related to this.","mosque"]],[["Let's consider traditions that change.",null]],[["Do you think traditional celebrations are becoming less important?","mosque"]],[["To a certain extent, yes, though I think it's the form that changes and not the importance.",null],["The visiting is the same.",null],["What's different is that a third of the people being visited are on a screen propped against something, and nobody in Damascus has decided yet whether that counts.","map"]],[["How do celebrations bring people together?","mosque"]],[["By making it something you have to do, mostly.",null],["I mean that seriously.",null],["The reason I see my second cousins in Damascus isn't that I'm fond of them.","family"],["It's that there's a day on which not appearing is noticed.",null],["Take that away and I'd see perhaps four of them.",null],["A custom like that is really just a date everybody has already agreed to.",null]],[["Should young people be encouraged to follow old traditions?",null]],[["Encouraged, yes.",null],["Required, no, and in my opinion, honestly, the difference is the whole question in Damascus.","map"],["Something that survives because people are made to do it has already stopped being the thing it was, hasn't it.",null],["My own view is that you show people why it mattered and then you've got to accept the answer.",null]],[["Now let's talk about what a country keeps.","map"]],[["What should a country try to preserve from its past?","map"]],[["The things that can't be rebuilt, I would say.",null],["Well, buildings can be put back up.","stairs"],["We've seen that in Syria, and it's worth doing.","map"],["But a way of making something, or a way of speaking, that only exists in people.",null],["Once the last person who does it properly has gone, that's not recoverable at any price.","money"]],[["Who should be responsible for protecting culture?",null]],[["It's a double-edged sword, really, because, no, that's not the right expression.",null],["What I mean is there are two sides.",null],["If the government in Damascus does it you get museums, which is honest but a bit dead.","map"],["If you leave it to people, you only keep what's popular.",null],["I don't think either one on its own is enough.",null]],[["Will young people's traditions be different in fifty years?",null]],[["Certainly, and I think the ones that last will be the ones nobody planned.",null],["The things my generation does that will still be happening aren't the official ones.","generations"],["They'll be some small habit around a phone that nobody in Damascus has named yet.","map"],["That's usually how it has worked before.",null]],[["Thank you.",null],["That is the end of the speaking test.","exam"]]]});
